===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|rare|transitive}} To [[address]] as “mademoiselle”.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Auguste de Lacroix; [unknown, transl.]|chapter=The Parisian Lady’s Maid|title=Pictures of the French: A Series of Literary and Graphic Delineations of French Character|location=London|publisher=W[illia]m S. Orr and Co.,{{nb...|Paternoster Row}}|year=1840|page=235|passage=If not courted by some tall and handsome “Chasseur,” or some crafty little valet who “'''mademoiselles'''” her, she almost invariably condescends to notice favourably some dandified linendraper’s assistant, or sixth clerk in an attorney’s office, whom she met one holiday at the Chaumière or at the Ermitage.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Fredrika Bremer|tlr=&#91;{{w|Mary Howitt}}&#93;|chapter=The Education of Women|title=The President’s Daughters. A Narrative of a Governess.|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher={{...|Published by}} James Munroe and Company,{{nb...|134 Washington Street}}|year=1843|page=57|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/presidentsdaugh00bremgoog/page/n67/mode/1up|column=1|passage=“And if Edla should have a taste and inclination for a military life, would, Mademoiselle wish to made her a general? or, if she had a particular ''penchant'' for anatomy, an anatomical professor? Young ladies belong probably to the St. Simonians, and young ladies like these desire that everywhere in civil life the woman should have the same privileges as the man.” (The President always '''Mademoiselled''' me very much when he was displeased with me.)}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=[[w:Dwight's Journal of Music|Dwight’s Journal of Music: A Paper of Art and Literature]]|volume=III|issue=24|location=Boston, Mass.|date=17 September 1853|page=191|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_dwights-journal-of-music-a-paper-of-art-and-literature_1853-09-17_3_24/page/191/mode/1up|column=3|passage=A lady, whom the bills—neither Missing, Madaming, nor '''Mademoiselling'''—entitled Adelaide Weinthal, appeared as Maffeo Orsini.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:George du Maurier|title=[[w:Trilby (novel)|Trilby]]|journal=[[w:Harper's Magazine|Harper’s New Monthly Magazine]]|volume=LXXXVIII|issue=DXXIV|month=January|year=1894|page=186|column=1|passage=There were ladies too, ''en cheveux'', in caps and bonnets, some of whom knew Trilby, and thee’d and thou’d with familiar affection, while others '''mademoiselle’d''' her with distant politeness, and were '''mademoiselle’d''' and madame’d back again.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=L. McManus &#91;{{w|Charlotte Elizabeth McManus}}&#93;|chapter=II|title=Lally of the Brigade: A Romance|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Louis Coues Page|L[ouis] C[oues] Page]] and Company|year=1899|pages=[https://archive.org/details/lallybrigade00macmgoog/page/n38/mode/1up 24] and [https://archive.org/details/lallybrigade00macmgoog/page/n44/mode/1up 30]|passage=“Mademoiselle, let me mount you on my mare,” I said, “and while you ride on I will get your horse.” She gave a little gasp. “You must not '''mademoiselle''' me,” she exclaimed; {{...}} “Mademoiselle,” I said, “it is imperative that we leave the high road. We are, I fear, pursued, and we must throw our pursuers off our track.” “I wish you would not '''mademoiselle''' me,” she pouted, dragging on the mare’s mouth.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Gael Greene|title=Bite: A New York Restaurant Strategy for Hedonists, Masochists, Selective Penny Pinchers and the Upwardly Mobile|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:W. W. Norton & Company|W. W. Norton & Company, Inc.]]|year=1971|page=60|isbn=	0 393 08635 6|passage=Pamela was not the least bit impressed by the chiaroscuro of attendants '''mademoiselling''' her.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Carol Thurston|title=Flair|location=London|publisher={{w|Pan Books}}|year=1987|year_published=1989|page=26|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/flair0000thur/page/26/mode/1up|isbn=0 330 30599 9|passage=First you thought I was a French schoolgirl, and '''mademoiselled''' me all over the place, and tonight I look different from the ‘girl’ in chausses you met this afternoon.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Bud Egeland|title=A Matter of Time|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Vantage Press|Vantage Press, Inc.]]|year=1998|page=86|isbn=0-533-12689-4|passage=“I don’t think so, mademoiselle. Irma will not like.” “Don’t '''mademoiselle''' me, Pierre, I know all about you guys.”}}

